---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { BUY_URL, PRICE, FOUNDING_PRICE, COMPARISON } from "../lib/product.mjs";
---

<Layout
  title="Mac disk space analyzer compared — DiskPrune, DaisyDisk, DiskBuddy, DissectMac"
  description="A Mac disk space analyzer should explain why the volume is full, then move only what you approve to Trash. How DiskPrune compares to DaisyDisk, DiskBuddy, DissectMac, and CleanMyMac."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-site px-6 py-16">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted"><a href="/">DiskPrune</a> · Compare</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight">
      Mac disk space analyzers, compared without the launch candy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Most people searching this are not shopping for eight charts. They have a
      80 GB System Data number, a Docker.raw they cannot explain, or DerivedData
      from last year’s Xcode. Here is what each tool actually is.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-12 max-w-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The short version</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">DaisyDisk</strong> — best sunburst on Mac. ~$9.99, 5 Macs, notarized. Weak on why a byte exists. Collector cleanup.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">DiskBuddy</strong> — eight visualizations, lifetime license, launch pricing. Unsigned as of September 2026. Packaging is the product.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">DissectMac</strong> — free notarized treemap. No DiskPrune-style cleanup path.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">CleanMyMac</strong> — subscription suite. Aggressive cleanup marketing. See <a href="/vs-cleanmymac">DiskPrune vs CleanMyMac</a>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">DiskPrune</strong> — storage autopsy, named developer locations, plan → dry run → Trash only. {PRICE} lifetime ({FOUNDING_PRICE} founding). Notarized.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Table</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[52rem] text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
              <th class="sticky left-0 bg-paper py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-muted">Feature</th>
              {COMPARISON.columns.map((col) => (
                <th class:list={["py-3 px-3 font-semibold", col.id === "diskprune" ? "text-ink" : "text-muted"]}>
                  {col.name}
                </th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {COMPARISON.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline align-top">
                <th class="sticky left-0 bg-paper py-3 pr-4 font-medium">{row.feature}</th>
                {COMPARISON.columns.map((col) => (
                  <td class:list={["py-3 px-3", col.id === "diskprune" ? "text-ink" : "text-muted"]}>
                    {row[col.id]}
                  </td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-page mt-12 max-w-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What “analyzer” should mean</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        A sunburst is a map of size. A treemap is a map of size. Eight charts are
        eight maps of size. None of them tell you that Docker.raw is a sparse image
        you must not trash, that a local snapshot is inspect-only, or that moving
        a folder to Trash has not yet changed available capacity.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        DiskPrune is built in the other direction: understand, then investigate,
        then review, then move to Trash. The Visual QA screenshots on the
        <a href="/">home page</a> are the native app, not a web demo.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
        If your only job is “show me the biggest folder as a daisy,” DaisyDisk
        already won that category and we will not clone it for v1.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={BUY_URL}>Buy DiskPrune — {FOUNDING_PRICE} <span class="opacity-60 line-through ml-1">{PRICE}</span></a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/download">Download</a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
